{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NN7GHGAoJQqGu63EXsNmLH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to expand detailed predicted data for each \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, based on the \"median\" tier definition. I need to generate realistic predicted results with rationales for why those results would occur under the median tier.\n\nLet me identify all planned experiments:\n- exp-001: DONE (not planned)\n- exp-002: planned\n- exp-006: cancelled\n- exp-007: planned\n- exp-003: DONE (not planned) — but status says \"planned\" even though status_note says Phase 1 done. The experiment plan says Phase 2 is needed. \n\nI'll treat this as planned since the status field indicates \"planned\" and Phase 2 is still needed. Looking through the rest: exp-004, exp-005, and exp-008 are all marked as planned. Let me verify the full list by checking the status field against what's actually in the experiment_plan, since there's a discrepancy between the registry showing some experiments as DONE and the plan showing them as planned.\n\nThe ones that are truly still pending are exp-002 for multi-benchmark analysis, exp-003 Phase 2 with perplexity features, exp-004 for attention weights, exp-005 for architecture expansion, exp-007 for the 340M rebound mechanism, exp-008 for statistical robustness, and exp-010 for convergence-divergence analysis. I should focus on generating predictions for these remaining experiments rather than the ones already completed in the registry.\n\nFor exp-003 Phase 2 specifically, the median tier expectation is that perplexity will show only marginal statistical significance in one architecture while topic and domain features won't be significant at all. Now I'm going through each of the remaining experiments to construct median-tier predictions based on what we'd expect to see.\n\nLooking at the key metrics that define the median tier, I'm seeing ranges for things like the standard deviation reduction across LAMBADA (70-85%), the dose-response correlation across benchmarks (around -0.75 to -0.55), and whether we get significance in just one or two benchmarks. For the attention pattern analysis, I'd expect a modest correlation with the effect size around 0.15 to 0.3. The bootstrap confidence intervals should show the lower bound somewhere between 0.25 and 0.55 for the standard deviation reduction.\n\nThe core assumption here is that exp-002 will show ARC-Easy is too noisy to extract clear patterns, while HellaSwag confirms the non-convergence pattern we're tracking.\n\nNow looking at the follow-up experiments: exp-003 shows perplexity as a marginal signal in Phase 2 with only one architecture reaching significance, while the topic and domain features don't pan out—so the mechanism stays unclear but perplexity remains a suggestive lead. Exp-004's attention correlations fall short of the preregistered threshold despite some interesting qualitative patterns. Exp-008 shows the GDN-excluded convergence holds directionally but with wider confidence intervals that shrink the effect sizes. And exp-007 reveals the 340M rebound is partially explained by overlap with harder samples, though there's a confounding factor with the training token count difference between the two conditions. Now I'm working through the remaining experiments. For exp-007, the rebound effect has a partial explanation but the training token confound remains unresolved. In exp-008, the bootstrap confidence intervals are quite wide, and while the GDN-excluded claims hold directionally, the effect sizes diminish. For exp-010, I'm seeing co-evolution demonstrated across the architectures, though the correlation strength is moderate rather than strong.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11498,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_in